Golden boot log -- zynqmp_apu, fault-injection configuration
============================================================

Companion to boot_log.golden, covering the CONFIG_AD9081_FAULT_INJECTION=y
build. Captured from the ZCU102 A53 console, hardware-verified.

Four injected faults produce ten FI checks, run after a normal bring-up
completes. All ten report FI PASS here; any FI FAIL line, or a missing FI PASS,
is a regression.

(PLAN_driver_model.md and HANDOFF_noos_compare.md say "8/8 checks". That count
is stale -- fault_injection.c has ten fi_pass() call sites, and ten fire on
hardware. The two extra are teardown-effect and watchdog-no-false-positive,
added after the handoff was written.)

Provenance
----------
Zephyr build : v4.4.0-7880-g90a9cb34e850
Commit       : 90a9cb34e85 ("samples: ad9081: add the driver-model conversion
               plan") plus the uncommitted conversion of plan steps 1 and 6.
Tree state   : captured AFTER hmc7044 moved to
               drivers/clock_control/clock_control_hmc7044.c. This log is
               therefore the first hardware confirmation of the in-tree driver:
               its bring-up prefix matches boot_log.golden line for line, which
               is what the move was expected to change nothing about.
Board        : ZCU102, AD9082 (prod_id 0x9082, grade 0x2, rev 0x3)
Bitstream    : zcu102_ad9081_m8_l4, built 2026/07/12 -- M8/L4/F4/K32/S1/NP16,
               TX link-mode 9, RX link-mode 10, 8B10B subclass 1
Config       : prj.conf + fault_injection.conf (CONFIG_AD9081_FAULT_INJECTION=y)
Binary       : 153000 bytes (vs 148792 for the default build)

How to capture a comparable log
-------------------------------
    west build -p always -b zynqmp_apu samples/boards/amd/ad9081_chipid \
        -d build_fi -- -DEXTRA_CONF_FILE=fault_injection.conf
    # flash the PL bitstream, then from u-boot:
    #   go <load addr>    (with zephyr.bin -- not bootelf)

Reading a diff against this file
--------------------------------
Timestamps and addresses vary run to run. Two more things vary legitimately and
are not regressions:

  * TPL clk_freq alternates between 0x00027fff and 0x00028000 across otherwise
    identical bring-ups -- it is a measured ratio, off by one LSB depending on
    where the counter is sampled. boot_log.golden happens to show 0x00027fff on
    the adc-tpl line where this log shows 0x00028000.

  * The <wrn> and <err> lines under FI 1 and FI 4 are the point of the test, not
    a problem. "fi-bad: link_init ... failed (-5)" is a synthetic device that
    returns -EIO by design; "GT PLL not locked after 2x100ms" is the RX GT being
    deliberately pointed at an undriven QPLL1. A run with no warnings at all
    would mean the faults were not injected.

Everything above "=== JESD204 fault injection: 4 faults ===" is the ordinary
bring-up and is covered by boot_log.golden's own criteria.

What each fault establishes
---------------------------
FI 1  fsm-accounting             A phase whose per-device and per-link ops both
                                 fail counts every failure (3 across two
                                 phases) and the walk still reaches
                                 link_running instead of aborting early.
      fsm-reverse-walk           Teardown walks the synthetic topology in
                                 reverse with no failures.

FI 2  lane-desync                A lane status of 0x30 -- upper bits plausible,
                                 state field cleared -- makes the RX watchdog
                                 return -EAGAIN and bounce the link. Healthy
                                 lanes on this board read 0x32.
      lane-desync-recovery       The bounce is a real LINK_DISABLE cycle, and
                                 the link renegotiates back to DATA unaided.
      watchdog-no-false-positive With nothing forced, the watchdog is silent.
                                 This is the regression test for the
                                 inverted-polarity bug that bounced working
                                 links.

FI 3  teardown                   The teardown walk completes with no failed
                                 steps.
      teardown-effect            Teardown actually does something: the link
                                 leaves DATA. (It disables the chip's JRX
                                 deframer and nothing else.)
      teardown-rebringup         The previously-unverified case -- the link is
                                 re-bringable. GT, FPGA lane clocks and the
                                 HMC7044 tree stay running, so this re-runs
                                 CLK_SYNC and LINK_ENABLE over live clocks.

FI 4  gt-refclk-lost             Pointing the RX GT at an undriven QPLL1 yields
                                 -ETIMEDOUT after exactly two bounded 100 ms
                                 reset attempts -- the retry loop terminates
                                 rather than spinning. STATUS reads 0x00000010.
      gt-refclk-restore          Restoring CPLL rebuilds the link to DATA, so
                                 the failed attempt left no latched state
                                 behind.

Known-good values worth checking explicitly
-------------------------------------------
  10x "FI PASS", zero "FI FAIL"
  FI 1: exactly 3 counted failures (1 in link_init, 2 in link_setup)
  FI 2: lanes read 0x00000032 healthy; watchdog -EAGAIN on forced 0x30
  FI 4: rx_adxcvr STATUS 0x00000010 after the failed lock; CONTROL 0x1025
        while broken, 0x1005 once restored
  Three full bring-ups reach DATA (initial, post-teardown, post-refclk-restore),
  each with chip JTX 0x007d / JRX 0x000f and AXI tx 0x13 / rx 0x03

